3.1 pop services
================

‘pop_services’ is a list of services to be started for the scenario.

   A service is defined by:

   ‘id’ (required, string) – A unique identifier for the service within
the scenario.  A common practice is to use a kebab-case version of the
service name.

   ‘dir’ (required, string) – The working directory (pwd) from which the
service process should be started.  This can be an absolute path or a
path starting with ‘$HOME’, which will be expanded to an absolute path.

   ‘base_url’ (required, string) – The URL where the service’s HTTP
endpoint is reachable.

   ‘start_cmd_args’ (required, list of strings) – The command(s) needed
to start the service.  These will be executed via a shell interpreter
(default: ‘bash -c’).  For example, if ‘make run’ is specified, the
system will actually execute ‘bash -c make run’.

   ‘shell_path’ (optional, string) - the path to the shell interpreter
to use to run the service.  This defaults to ‘bash’.

   ‘env_vars’ (optional, map of string to string) – A set of environment
variables to be defined for the service.

   ‘health_check’ (required, object) – Defines how the system determines
whether the service is healthy.

   Health checks can take different forms:

   Dummy/Placebo health check:

   ‘always_healthy’ (boolean) – If set, forces the service to always be
considered healthy (or never healthy).

   HTTP health check:

   ‘path’ (required, string) – The HTTP route used to check the
service’s health (e.g., ‘/health’).

   ‘patience_seconds’ (required, integer) – The maximum time allowed for
the service to become healthy.  If the service is healthy before this
deadline, the scenario setup continues without waiting.


